/*****************************************************************************
    kant8T -- CHARACTERISING WHAT THE FRAME DISCIPLINE LOSES. 2026-08-05.

    ⚠ THIS FIXTURE CHARACTERISES, IT DOES NOT FIX. CLAIM KANT-8's repair is
    TONY'S -- both candidate fixes touch the interpreter's hot path. The job
    here is to establish, by measurement, exactly WHICH returns survive a
    recursive action and which do not, so that a frame-model decision is taken
    against a diagnosed disease rather than an inferred one.

    THE MECHANISM, read off GroupActions.rtn and not inferred:
        if field.recursive      saveLocalFields(field);
        result  = processAction(field);
        if field.recursive      restoreLocalFields(field);
        return  result;
    saveLocalFields copies each (isArgument || isLocal) && !noPrint body onto a
    stack and BLANKS it -- except an argument, which it saves WITHOUT blanking.
    restoreLocalFields pops each body back with `*groupBody = *body`, an IN-PLACE
    overwrite of the node's contents. So a returned node is a POINTER whose
    CONTENTS are reverted underneath the caller. That is the whole disease.

    FOUR ROWS, each isolating ONE variable:

      K1  recursive, returns a LOCAL      -- KANT-8 as claimed. The baseline.
      K2  recursive, returns its ARGUMENT -- ⚠ THE NAMED OPEN PROBE, unresolved
          since 2026-07-29: the corpus says "Not verified whether returning the
          ARGUMENT instead of a local dodges it; that is the obvious next
          probe." saveLocalFields does NOT blank an argument, but restore still
          pops a body back over it, so the answer is not readable from the
          blanking asymmetry alone.
      K3  NON-recursive, returns a local  -- ⚠ THE CONTROL, and the row that
          makes K1 mean anything. Without it, "returning a local is broken"
          cannot be told from "`recursive` is the discriminator". K3's body must
          not name itself, or the parse-time inference arms it.
      K4  recursive, returns a local, RECURSIVE BRANCH NEVER TAKEN -- asks
          whether the tax is paid on EVERY call or only on calls that actually
          recurse. `recursive` is a STATIC flag set at parse time by identity,
          so the prediction is that K4 breaks identically to K1 with no
          recursion occurring at all. If so, the cost is not "recursion is
          expensive" but "naming yourself anywhere in your body is".

    ⚠ K1 vs K3 IS THE ANTI-VACUITY PAIR. Both rows returning a bare tag would be
    consistent with "return is broken generally", which would say nothing about
    frames. K3 must come back with a REAL VALUE for K1's emptiness to indict the
    save/restore bracket specifically.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (standing harness rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(Kant8);
define
    k1depth;
    k2depth;
    k4depth;
    kOut;
    kArg;

    k1 argument code={
        k1loc = 42;
        if k1depth > 0;
            k1depth = k1depth - 1;
            k1(argument);
        return k1loc;
        };

    k2 argument code={
        if k2depth > 0;
            k2depth = k2depth - 1;
            k2(argument);
        return argument;
        };

    k3 code={
        k3loc = 42;
        return k3loc;
        };

    k4 argument code={
        k4loc = 42;
        if k4depth > 0;
            k4depth = k4depth - 1;
            k4(argument);
        return k4loc;
        };

    /*  ===================== K5 (2026-08-05) ==========================
        INVOCATION-HISTORY DEPENDENCE. k5 is k1's body character for character,
        under its own name so that "call 1" is a genuine FIRST invocation --
        k1 has already been called by the K1 row above, and the whole question
        is whether the FIRST call differs from the SECOND.
        Basis: restoreLocalFields clears `recursive` on the outermost restore
        when the stack came back empty (GroupActions.rtn:587), so call 2 may
        skip the bracket entirely and return the local intact.  */
    k5depth;
    k5 argument code={
        k5loc = 42;
        if k5depth > 0;
            k5depth = k5depth - 1;
            k5(argument);
        return k5loc;
        };

    /*  ===================== K6 (2026-08-05) ==========================
        MUTUAL RECURSION -- THE UNPROTECTED SIBLING. `recursive` is set only on
        DIRECT self-mention (ruleActions.rtn:1310), so in A -> B -> A NEITHER A
        NOR B trips the bracket: no save, no restore, no protection at all.
        The carried thing is an ITERATOR CURSOR mid-walk, which is the proven
        node-resident specimen class (it is what displayForm's divergence was).

        ⚠ THE TWO NODES HAVE DIFFERENT ATTRIBUTE COUNTS ON PURPOSE. With one
        node both the protected and the trampled reading produce 3 and the row
        would discriminate nothing. Big has 3, small has 1:
            protected  outer walks its OWN cursor over big      -> 3
            trampled   inner re-points the SHARED cursor at small,
                       exhausts it, outer resumes on it          -> 1  */
    k6big       aa bb cc;
    k6small     xx;
    k6depth;
    k6A argument code={
        iterate k6cur on argument attributes;
        k6seen = 0;
        while ++k6cur;
            k6seen = k6seen + 1;
            if k6depth > 0;
                k6depth = k6depth - 1;
                k6B(k6small);
        return k6seen;
        };
    k6B argument code={
        k6A(argument);
        return 0;
        };

    /*  K6b -- THE CONTROL. Identical A -> B -> A shape, but the local is
        written entirely AFTER the B call returns and is never carried across
        it. Clean here proves K6a's failure is THE CARRY, not the shape.  */
    /*  ⚠ THE CONTROL ITERATES k6big BY NAME, NOT `argument`, AND THE FIRST CUT
        DID NOT -- which is how a SECOND trample was found. Written with
        `argument`, this control returned 1 instead of 3, because the inner
        activation had rebound the action's `argument` field to k6small and, with
        no bracket running, the OUTER resumed reading the inner's binding. That
        is a real result and it gets its own row (K6c) rather than being quietly
        designed out; here it would confound the iterator question with an
        argument question, and a control that fails for a second reason controls
        nothing.  */
    k6depth2;
    k6Actl argument code={
        if k6depth2 > 0;
            k6depth2 = k6depth2 - 1;
            k6Bctl(k6small);
        iterate k6cur2 on k6big attributes;
        k6seen2 = 0;
        while ++k6cur2;
            k6seen2 = k6seen2 + 1;
        return k6seen2;
        };

    /*  K6c -- THE ARGUMENT TRAMPLE, ISOLATED. No iterator at all: the outer
        simply reads its OWN argument's tag after the B call returns.
        ⚠ CONTRAST WITH K2, WHICH IS THE POINT. In DIRECT recursion an argument
        DODGES the emptying, because runAction binds before saveLocalFields runs
        so it is saved as-bound and restored as-bound. In MUTUAL recursion there
        is no bracket at all, so the argument is not restored -- it simply keeps
        the inner activation's binding. Same field, opposite outcomes, different
        mechanisms.  */
    k6depth3;
    k6Aarg argument code={
        if k6depth3 > 0;
            k6depth3 = k6depth3 - 1;
            k6Barg(k6small);
        return argument.taG;
        };
    k6Barg argument code={
        k6Aarg(argument);
        return 0;
        };

    /*  K6d -- THE RE-ITERATE, ISOLATED. No recursion, no mutual anything: one
        action, one cursor local, iterated twice on two different nodes. If the
        second walk counts 1 rather than 3, the cursor did not re-point and the
        whole K6b-first-draft result belongs to the iterator rather than to any
        frame question.  */
    k6Re argument code={
        iterate k6cur3 on k6small attributes;
        k6seen3 = 0;
        while ++k6cur3;
            k6seen3 = k6seen3 + 1;
        iterate k6cur3 on k6big attributes;
        k6seen3 = 0;
        while ++k6cur3;
            k6seen3 = k6seen3 + 1;
        return k6seen3;
        };
    k6Bctl argument code={
        k6Actl(argument);
        return 0;
        };

    /*  DRIVERS, so the jitted legs can run. testing() binds no argument, and
        these rows are about actions that TAKE one -- so each driver is nullary
        and makes the real call itself, exactly as dfDrive does for displayForm.
        Under jitting an ordinary call INLINES, so k1's body is emitted into the
        driver and its self-call is recognised through gJitInlining -- which is
        the path the frame bracket added, so this is a real parity test of it.  */
    k1Drive code={
        k1depth = 1;
        kOut = k1(kArg);
        };
    k2Drive code={
        k2depth = 1;
        kOut = k2(kArg);
        };
    ;

kArg = 7;

/*  ⚠⚠ THE JITTED PARITY LEGS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT RUN HERE, AND THE REASON IS
    A FINDING RATHER THAN A LIMITATION. They were written, run, and REMOVED:

      1. `return` UNDER JIT HAS NO EMITTER. It calls jitDegrade
         ("break/return under jit -- no emitter yet"). Since KANT-8 is a claim
         ABOUT WHAT A RETURN HANDS BACK, its jitted parity is not merely
         unanswered -- IT IS NOT YET ASKABLE. Every green rung on the ladder
         asserts a FIELD's value after the action, never a returned one, and
         this is why.
      2. Driving them CRASHED at 139, and the cause is a SEPARATE pre-existing
         defect that has nothing to do with return -- see incant/inlineSelfT,
         which isolates it. Running them here would put a SIGSEGV inside a
         fixture whose job is to characterise something else, and rule H5 exists
         because a fixture that dies takes every check below it with it.

    So this file stays INTERPRETED-ONLY and exits 0. The parity question is
    parked behind a named gate: it becomes askable when `return` gets an
    emitter.  */
print "== PARITY: jitted legs -- SEE HEADER, deliberately not run ==":;

print "== K3 CONTROL: NON-recursive action returning a local ==":;
kOut = k3();
print "K3 returned =" kOut " (want 42; if this is not 42 the fixture is void)":;

print "== K1 BASELINE: recursive action returning a local, one nested call ==":;
k1depth = 1;
kOut = k1(kArg);
print "K1 returned =" kOut " (want 42; KANT-8 predicts NOT 42)":;

print "== K4: recursive action, recursive branch NEVER TAKEN ==":;
k4depth = 0;
kOut = k4(kArg);
print "K4 returned =" kOut " (want 42; is the tax paid without recursing?)":;

print "== K2 OPEN PROBE: recursive action returning its ARGUMENT ==":;
k2depth = 1;
kOut = k2(kArg);
print "K2 returned =" kOut " (kArg is 7; does an argument dodge the restore?)":;

print "== K5: INVOCATION-HISTORY DEPENDENCE -- K1's shape, called TWICE ==":;
/*  ⚠ THE FLAG ITSELF CANNOT BE PRINTED FROM HERE, AND THAT IS A REPORTED
    CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. `recursive` is NOT in the GroupFields
    registry (incant/setup:236-276 lists 30-odd flags; recursivE is not among
    them), so there is no incant spelling for it -- and incant/flagProbe already
    established that a GroupFields name with no opDot case falls to
    `product.text = "access to ... not supported yet"`, a NON-EMPTY STRING that
    is TRUTHY either way. So an undeclared name would not merely fail, it would
    read as permanently set. Adding one is an interpreter-side edit and out of
    this brief's scope.
    ROUTED AROUND: the RETURNS are the flag's observable. The bracket's only
    effect on this shape is whether the local survives, so 42 means the bracket
    did not run and the tag means it did. Two calls, two readings.  */
k5depth = 1;
kOut = k5(kArg);
print "K5 call 1 returned =" kOut " (42 = bracket did NOT run; tag = it did)":;
k5depth = 1;
kOut = k5(kArg);
print "K5 call 2 returned =" kOut " (does invocation HISTORY change the answer?)":;

/*  ⚠ K5's ANSWER IS "NO", AND THE WHY IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN INFERRED --
    which matters, because a causal claim in this domain is a coin flip until it
    is run and this one can be READ instead.
    saveLocalFields pushes ONE saved body per field matching
    `(isArgument || isLocal) && !noPrint` (GroupActions.rtn:859-879);
    restoreLocalFields pops exactly as many, walking the same predicate backward
    (:589-593). So the stack is empty on entry to a restore IF AND ONLY IF the
    save pushed nothing -- i.e. the action has NO matching fields, in which case
    the bracket had nothing to blank in the first place.
    THEREFORE line 587's `if !recurseSTAK.length action.recursive = false;` IS
    REACHABLE ONLY FOR ACTIONS WHERE CLEARING IT CHANGES NOTHING. For any action
    the bracket actually affects, `recursive` is never cleared -- which is
    exactly what these two rows measure, and it makes the flag effectively
    static in practice even though it is written at run time.
    ⚠ THIS NARROWS A WARNING I ISSUED EARLIER THIS DAY. The S3 probe reported
    "recursive is cleared at run time, so compile results could depend on
    invocation history", and that was used as one of two reasons to reject
    option (c). The clause is TRUE but INERT for any action with a local or an
    argument. The ruling is unaffected -- (c)'s other reason was that `recursive`
    misses A->B->A entirely, and K6a/K6c below MEASURE that gap rather than
    predicting it -- but the record should carry the narrower claim.  */

print "== K6a: MUTUAL RECURSION A->B->A, node-resident local CARRIED across ==":;
k6depth = 1;
kOut = k6A(k6big);
print "K6a outer returned =" kOut " (3 = its own cursor; 1 = trampled by the inner)":;

print "== K6b CONTROL: same A->B->A, local written AFTER the B call ==":;
k6depth2 = 1;
kOut = k6Actl(k6big);
print "K6b outer returned =" kOut " (want 3; proves the SHAPE is not the fault)":;

print "== K6c: MUTUAL RECURSION -- the ARGUMENT, read after the B call ==":;
k6depth3 = 1;
kOut = k6Aarg(k6big);
print "K6c outer returned =" kOut " (k6big = its own; k6small = the inner's)":;

/*  ⚠⚠ K6d IS THE ELIMINATION ROW, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE A BAD WITNESS NEARLY
    INVERTED A DIAGNOSIS. K6b's first draft iterated `argument` and returned 1
    instead of 3; the attribution was "the inner activation trampled the
    argument". K6c was then written to check that -- and in its FIRST form it
    did `return argument;`, which printed 3, i.e. k6big's ATTRIBUTE COUNT rather
    than its identity. Read as "the argument is intact", that inverted the
    diagnosis and pointed at the iterator instead.
    ⚠ PRINTING A GROUP NODE PRINTS ITS ATTRIBUTE COUNT. It is a legal-looking
    number in the same range as the answer, so it does not announce itself as
    the wrong quantity. `return argument.taG;` is the honest witness and it says
    k6small -- the argument IS trampled, the original reading was right, and the
    thing that was briefly wrong was the instrument.
    K6d survives as the ELIMINATION it forced: a second `iterate` on the same
    cursor, two different nodes, one straight-line action, no recursion of any
    kind. It counts 3, so the re-iterate re-points correctly and the iterator is
    NOT a suspect in any row above. A control that eliminates is worth keeping
    even when the hypothesis it was built for dies.  */
print "== K6d: RE-ITERATE THE SAME CURSOR ON A DIFFERENT NODE, no recursion ==":;
kOut = k6Re(k6big);
print "K6d second walk counted =" kOut " (want 3; 1 = the re-iterate did NOT re-point)":;

print "kant8T SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
